/* ---- per diem explainer ---- */
VIEWS.perdiem=()=>`<section class="v7page"><div class="o7-wrap v7prose"><a class="backlink" href="${href("travel")}">${I("back",16)}Traveler calls</a><span class="eyebrow">${I("calc",14)} Per diem, explained plainly</span><h1>When is per diem really tax-free?</h1><p class="big muted">Short version: only when you have a real <b>tax home</b> you're paying for while you're away, and the job is <b>temporary</b>, meaning it's expected to last a year or less.</p>
 <div class="test7" role="note">${I("info",14)}<span><b>Not tax advice.</b> This is a plain-language summary of IRS rules so you know what to ask. Your situation is yours, so talk to a tax preparer who works with travelers.</span></div>
 <div class="v7cards3"><div class="card"><h3>${I("home",18)} 1. A real tax home</h3><p>Your tax home is your regular place of business. To count as "away," you need to keep up a home there: rent or a mortgage, utilities in your name, and you go back to it. Many locals ask for a lease or utility bill for the same reason.</p></div>
 <div class="card"><h3>${I("cal",18)} 2. The 1-year rule</h3><p>If a job is <b>realistically expected</b> to last more than a year, it's "indefinite" and per diem becomes taxable from day one. If a short call gets extended past a year, it becomes taxable from the day the expectation changed.</p></div>
 <div class="card"><h3>${I("receipt",18)} 3. Duplicate costs</h3><p>The point is that you're paying for two places. If you gave up your apartment and live in the RV full time with no other home, you're an "itinerant." Per diem is then generally taxable wages.</p></div></div>
 <div class="card"><h2>Quick check</h2><ul class="v7q">${[["Do you pay rent, a mortgage, or real expenses on a home you return to?","Required for tax-free per diem"],["Is the call expected to last under 12 months?","Over a year = indefinite = taxable"],["Do you keep ties there: voter card, vehicle registration, license, mail?","Helps show it's really home"],["Is the job more than about 50–60 miles from that home?","Many contractors and locals require this distance to pay per diem"]].map(([q,a])=>`<li><b>${q}</b><small class="muted">${a}</small></li>`).join("")}</ul></div>
 <div class="card"><h2>Ask before you accept</h2><p>Is per diem paid <b>7 days a week</b> or only days worked? Is it lost on a rain day or a missed day? Is it on a separate line (not baked into the rate)? Is there travel pay in and out? Sawyer calls show all of this up front.</p></div>
 <p class="small muted">Sources: <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p463" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Publication 463</a> · <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc511" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Topic 511, Business travel</a> · <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-wd/19-0003.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS info letter 2019-0003 (tax home)</a></p></div></section>`;
